# -> an optional closing line.
# Fixed: "a OT prophecy" -> "an OT prophecy" (17032); "telling him to find his loins" -> "gird up his loins"
# (17042, Job 40:7); "rhetorically;," -> "rhetorically;" and "he couldn't of he wanted to" -> "if he wanted to" (17042).

=== Introduction
--- Job 38–41 [title]
overline: The Job class · Part 4 of 5
heading: Job 38–41: The {Lord} Answers out of the Whirlwind
sub: Job is asking "why?" But God is showing him "Who."
refs: Job 38:1-4; Job 40:4; Job 41:10
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any gold reference to read the verse.
image: lessons/job/job-38-41-out-of-the-whirlwind.jpg | Shadow-play stage: a great whirlwind of light with the horse, the eagle, the ostrich, behemoth and leviathan around it, and Job kneeling small with his hand upon his mouth

--- Where we are
kicker: The Job class
heading: Part 4: Out of the whirlwind
tile: 1–2 | The trial begins
tile: 3–31 | Job and his three friends
tile: 32–37 | Elihu speaks
tile: 38–41 | **Out of the whirlwind**
tile: 42 | The latter end
p: The storm that gathered as Elihu spoke, which the boy used as it came to lecture Job with what he thought was profound wisdom, brings with it the voice of God. And He sets to immediately show the shallowness of Elihu and the three friends' thinking.

=== Where wast thou?
--- Then the Lord answered Job
kicker: Job 38 · The {Lord}
big: Job 38:1-3 | mark: Then the LORD answered Job out of the whirlwind; Who is this that darkeneth counsel by words without knowledge?
p: What a terrifying moment, to know that God will demand answers of you.
p: And this fear should grip all men, that they believe on Christ, to avoid that fierce judgment, where all will be asked of those not found in His book of Life, by the omnipotent God of all.

--- The corner stone thereof
kicker: Job 38 · The {Lord}
big: Job 38:4-6 | mark: Where wast thou when I laid the foundations of the earth?; who laid the corner stone thereof
p: He laid the foundations of the earth, the breadth and scope of creation was measured out by Him. The foundations of the world are set by Him, and the cornerstone thereof. [[Ephesians 2:20]] [[1 Peter 2:6-8]]
p: The morning stars sang together, and all the sons of God shouted for joy and the truth of Jesus Christ, the lamb slain before the foundation of the world. The cornerstone laid, upon Which all is built.

--- The sons of God shouted for joy
kicker: Job 38 · The {Lord}
big: Job 38:7 | mark: the morning stars sang together; all the sons of God shouted for joy
p: (I believe this is an OT prophecy of Christ, Who has been since before creation, but Who IS and was born of men, to die and rise again. That the stars are the hosts of Heaven. That the sons of God are those from Adam who believed.)
p: "Morning stars" are angels, distinct from "sons of God" ([[Hebrews 1:5]]), who are believers rejoicing in Christ's day, like Abraham did ([[John 8:56]]). This happens outside time, reflecting Christ's eternal victory ([[Revelation 13:8]]), not a pre-human event.
read: app:doctrine-angels~15859 | The sons of God are believers, never angels

--- Time itself is a testimony
kicker: Job 38 · The {Lord}
big: Job 38:12 | mark: Hast thou commanded the morning since thy days
p: From the seas to the shores to the heights of the sky, to the position of the stars in the sky ([[Job 38:31-33]]), God has set all things, and the paths thereof.
p: The wonder of creation is not only in the placement of these things God created, but in their paths and purposes. Time itself is a testimony of His power. The day chasing away the darkness ([[Job 38:12-15]]). The rivers carrying the floodwaters to the sea ([[Job 38:8-11]]). The voice of thunder, and the rain upon the wilderness, making it spring to life ([[Job 38:25-27]]).

--- Only God can create these things
kicker: Job 38 · The {Lord}
p: The pictures of God's mercy and wrath, revealed. Made manifest that no man has an excuse. Only God can create these things, and not as man would, even if we could. For the Lord knows the beginning from the end. The order He has made is greater than our ability to comprehend.
p: Creation isn't like the birth of man. Flood waters are pictured in birth, but is not the birth. There is no womb that could create the frost.

--- The treasures of the hail
kicker: Job 38 · The {Lord}
big: Job 38:22-23 | mark: the treasures of the hail, Which I have reserved against the time of trouble
p: The hail which will be used in due time by the Lord in His wrath. Rain isn't called and lightning ordered and arrayed, but by the living God.
p: God has even put what we call the food chain in its place, that the beasts of the world eat, even as they don't sow or reap. But that He opens death to all, that they may eat.
take: And this same God puts wisdom in man's heart. [[Job 38:36]]

=== The beasts of His providence
--- The big picture
kicker: Job 39 · The {Lord}
lead: The Lord continues to speak to Job out of the whirlwind. Job, His servant, seeks to know why he has been afflicted. But God challenges him to see the things that His providence delivers. The grand scheme. The big picture.
p: That for all the wisdom the Lord has given man, and the ability of man to apply it for their lives, there is so much more that is beyond our control, but not His.

--- The wild goats
kicker: Job 39 · The {Lord}
big: Job 39:1 | mark: Knowest thou the time when the wild goats of the rock bring forth?
p: God asks Job to consider the wild goats, the wild ass, the unicorn - which isn't a one horned mythical beast, but the wild oxen, the strong bull or aurochs as it was known, the ostrich or the horse. The hawk and the eagle.
p: Man doesn't control or understand the time of the goat, when they calve. Unlike the tame goats, they, without any aid or midwifery, bring forth their kids in sorrow, and cast them out to fend for themselves. And God provides. [[Job 39:1-4]]

--- The wild ass and the unicorn
kicker: Job 39 · The {Lord}
big: Job 39:5 | mark: Who hath sent out the wild ass free?
p: The wild ass is free. God has given it liberty in the wilderness, and a stubborn rebellion against being controlled by men. He searches the mountains for his food, and while it doesn't starve, it isn't fed as a tamed donkey would be. [[Job 39:5-8]]
p: The unicorn, or wild bull, doesn't serve man, or tread out the corn. It doesn't reside in a stable. It can't be trusted with the work of the tamed oxen. But it lives. [[Job 39:9-12]]

--- The peacock and the ostrich
kicker: Job 39 · The {Lord}
scripture: Job 39:13-17 | mark: God hath deprived her of wisdom
p: God gives goodly wings to the peacock, which cannot fly. And wings and feathers to the ostrich, which not only cannot fly, but runs in the wilderness, dropping her eggs to be hatched in the sand and sun. Not protected in a nest, but exposed to being crushed or broken and eaten.
p: And yet, they don't go extinct. God provides, even as He withheld wisdom and understanding to the big dumb bird, giving her speed to outrun man, even on horseback.

--- The horse
kicker: Job 39 · The {Lord}
big: Job 39:19 | mark: Hast thou given the horse strength?
p: The ways and whys of creation don't always make sense to man, and are often well out of our control, and yet it continues on, in His ways.
p: The horse, whose strength man has harnessed, did not gain its strength from men. It can be startled by a grasshopper, but charge into battle, the noise of armed men, and the shield and spear rattling against him, the trumpet sounding as it rides headlong towards death. [[Job 39:19-25]]

--- The hawk and the eagle
kicker: Job 39 · The {Lord}
big: Job 39:26-27 | mark: Doth the hawk fly by thy wisdom; Doth the eagle mount up at thy command
p: The hawk flies towards the south, soaring in the warm winds, without our instruction. The eagle nests in the castles of mountains, and seeks prey afar off. Not by will of man. Her chicks go where corpses lay, and feast. [[Job 39:26-30]]
p: Like the beasts, man is a wild thing, marrying, and bringing forth children. Stubborn and strong willed. Even at liberty, prone to wander, God provides.
take: In all this, man is helpless. It is all the providence of God.

--- We are like these same beasts
kicker: Job 39 · The {Lord}
p: But we are like these same beasts. We go about our lives, having children that will grow and go off. Stubborn to instruction, refusing to be driven where we should go. Forsaking the blessings of being a servant to God, in exchange for His mercies and bare sustenance in the wilderness.
p: We behave in ways that should result in our extinction. And yet, God's mercies are extended to us in ways we don't even notice or comprehend.

--- It is all in order
kicker: Job 39 · The {Lord}
p: Man, who is frightened of thunder and who can't be trusted to do what's right, who runs after war, who abandons their own kin to the wastes of life, who feeds on the dead, our wisdom is not sufficient to explain our nature, or how it mirrors those wild beasts we can't comprehend. But to God, and to His point of view, it is all in order.

--- Job asks why, God shows Who
kicker: Job 39 · The {Lord}
p: In all these, suffering, danger, apparent neglect, and death are woven into the fabric of life, yet the creatures flourish under God's hand. None of it is explained to them. None of it is micromanaged by man. And none of it indicates that God has abandoned His creation.
take: Job is asking "why?" But God is showing him "Who." By enlarging Job's view of God, and the order He created, He is answering Job, just not in the way Job intended.
read: app:doctrine-creation~17040 | Job asks why, God shows Who

=== I will lay mine hand upon my mouth
--- Behold, I am vile
kicker: Job 40 · Job answers
big: Job 40:4-5 | mark: Behold, I am vile; what shall I answer thee? I will lay mine hand upon my mouth
p: The Lord asks from the whirlwind if any contend with Him, do so. If any seek to instruct Him. Job has no answer. His friends are silent. [[Job 40:2]]
p: In the face of the living God, righteous Job faced with the holiness that is the Lord, is speechless. God responds to meek Job rhetorically; telling him to gird up his loins, to stand up and go against Him. To condemn Him and declare himself righteous.

--- Hast thou an arm like God?
kicker: Job 40 · The {Lord}
scripture: Job 40:7-14 | mark: Hast thou an arm like God?; thine own right hand can save thee
p: That if Job is strong, having a mighty arm, a voice like thunder, decked in the majesty and glory like God, and if Job can abase the proud in their wrath, go on and stand against God.
p: Now, as we know from the beginning and the end, Job maintained his integrity and his faith. He did not stand to correct God. But God says he couldn't if he wanted to. He isn't like unto man. And our own right hand cannot save us. Only His. [[Hebrews 12:2]]

--- Behold now behemoth
kicker: Job 40 · The {Lord}
scripture: Job 40:15-19 | mark: Behold now behemoth, which I made with thee; he that made him can make his sword to approach unto him
p: Having made clear the lowliness of man, God again points to His creation, how can man ever comprehend the wonder and glory of God and His divine will, when they can't even comprehend the creatures and their ways He created.
p: He describes the behemoth, a creature like we are, whose great size and stature is achieved, while eating grass as an ox. But its strength and power is overwhelming. A tail like a cedar. His hide, even around its privates, is strong, bones like brass, like bars of iron.

--- Only God could make it
kicker: Job 40 · The {Lord}
p: There is discussion about what this creature is. A hippo, rhino, an elephant, a brontosaurus. But the number of beasts throughout the time since creation it could apply to, points to a real animal, one Job would know. Irrelevant to the main point, that only God could make it, fell it, feed it.
take: To God, this creature is nothing, while it is a behemoth to man.

=== Leviathan
--- Canst thou draw out leviathan?
kicker: Job 41 · The {Lord}
scripture: Job 41:1-4 | mark: Canst thou draw out leviathan with an hook?; Will he make a covenant with thee?
p: God again humbles Job. The great leviathan, that dragon of many heads, whose size and power make him impossible to catch, his scales like armor, his breath as fire that even his sneezing brings forth light ([[Job 41:18]]).
p: Again, like the behemoth, there is much debate over what animal the leviathan is, a whale, a crocodile, some other mythic beast.

--- That old serpent
kicker: Job 41 · The {Lord}
scripture: Psalm 74:13-14; Isaiah 27:1-2 | mark: thou brakest the heads of the dragons in the waters; leviathan the piercing serpent, even leviathan that crooked serpent
p: But the use of the term, interchangeable with dragon throughout scripture, makes a compelling case that it is that old serpent... *he is a king over all the children of pride.* ([[Job 41:34]]; [[Psalm 104:25-26]]; [[Revelation 12:3-9]]; [[Revelation 13:4]]; [[Revelation 20:1-2]]; [[Revelation 20:10]])
p: But, regardless if it is a real beast, a figure for Satan, both...the insignificant Job is powerless before it. Man cannot contain it. Only God can break its heads and slay it.

--- Who can stand before Him?
kicker: Job 41 · The {Lord}
big: Job 41:10 | mark: who then is able to stand before me?
p: In Job 41, God illustrates human inability to subdue the leviathan. No one can catch it like a small fish using a hook, line, or thorn through its gills. It will not plead for mercy or agree to serve as a captive. It cannot be tamed or confined like a pet bird for amusement. Any hope of capturing it is vain; the mere sight of it overwhelms even brave men, causing them to faint.
take: Man is finite. God, infinite. And before Him who can stand?

--- Man is helpless against sin
kicker: Job 41 · The {Lord}
p: If the leviathan is a picture (real or spiritually, or again, both) of Satan and of the sin of this world, isn't it a fitting analogy?
p: Man is helpless against sin on our own. We can't subdue it, reason with it, enter into a covenant to make peace with it, control it, enslave it, or truly make profit from it. It produces fire that burns all in its path, leaving behind it a trail that shines behind it.

--- A kind of closure
kicker: Job 41 · The {Lord}
p: Satan himself initiated the trial of Job, that the old serpent could accuse the man God described as perfect in his ways. And since the destruction of Job's life, his wealth, posterity and health, the Devil is noticeably absent afterwards.
p: There is a kind of closure to the story here, in the penultimate chapter, to see God describe a beast too fierce for man to handle, yet nothing to Him. Just as man is incapable of destroying sin and death and the fires of Hell, himself.

--- Only God can subdue the dragon
kicker: Job 41 · The {Lord}
p: Job isn't restored by his own wisdom, his works, by proving his own righteousness before God. When the Lord offered Job to gird up his loins and confront Him, Job could only cover his mouth and sit in silence. So, it is God who restores Job.
take: Only by God's grace do we find a way to overcome the world, death and Hell, by the blood of the atonement shed by Jesus, and everlasting glory by His life, risen in triumph.
read: app:doctrine-salvation~17056 | Only God can subdue the dragon

=== What we have seen
--- What we have seen
point: Out of the whirlwind | The storm that gathered as Elihu spoke brings with it the voice of God.
point: The corner stone | The cornerstone laid, upon Which all is built.
point: Why, and Who | Job is asking "why?" But God is showing him "Who."
point: Our own right hand | Our own right hand cannot save us. Only His.
point: The dragon | Only God can subdue the dragon. Man is helpless against it.

